.TH LOGANALYZER 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
loganalyzer \- count lines, words and log levels in a log file using mmap()
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loganalyzer
[\fB\-k\fR \fIkeyword\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-h\fR] \fIlogfile\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loganalyzer
maps the whole log file into memory with
.BR mmap (2)
and scans it once, line by line. It reports the total number of lines
and words.
.PP
The command is part of the Custom Shell. Its entry point is
.BR loganalyzer_main(argc,\ argv) ,
which the shell calls when the user types
.BR loganalyzer .
It must be built on Linux (or WSL), because
.I sys/mman.h
is not available on Windows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-k " keyword"
Count the lines that contain \fIkeyword\fR (case-sensitive).
.TP
.B \-t
Count lines by log level: ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG. Only the first
level found in a line is counted.
.TP
.B \-h
Print help and exit.
.SH SIGNALS
Pressing Ctrl+C (SIGINT) during a scan stops it and prints
.B [Interrupted by Ctrl+C - partial results]
followed by the counts so far.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including an empty file.
.TP
.B 1
Error: bad option, missing \fB\-k\fR word, no file given, or the file
could not be opened.
.TP
.B 130
Stopped by Ctrl+C.
.SH TEST FILES
Make these in the terminal before starting the Custom Shell.
.TP
.B sample.log
12 lines (line 6 is empty):
.PP
.nf
.RS
2026-11-01 10:00:01 INFO Server started on port 8080
2026-11-01 10:00:05 DEBUG Loading config file /etc/app.conf
2026-11-01 10:01:12 INFO User alice logged in
2026-11-01 10:02:30 WARN Disk usage at 85%
2026-11-01 10:03:44 ERROR Database connection timeout

2026-11-01 10:04:10 INFO Retrying database connection
2026-11-01 10:04:15 ERROR Database connection timeout
2026-11-01 10:05:00 INFO User bob logged in
2026-11-01 10:06:21 WARN Slow response: 2300 ms
2026-11-01 10:07:02 DEBUG Cache hit ratio 0.92
2026-11-01 10:08:59 INFO User alice logged out
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B empty.log
An empty file, made with: \fBtouch empty.log\fR
.SH TESTS AND EXPECTED OUTPUT
.SS 1. Basic stats
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Total words      : 75
.RE
.fi
.SS 2. Log levels
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-t sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Total words      : 75
\-\-\- Log levels \-\-\-
  ERROR  : 2
  WARN   : 2
  INFO   : 5
  DEBUG  : 2
.RE
.fi
.SS 3. Keyword
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-k timeout sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Total words      : 75
Lines with "timeout" : 2
.RE
.fi
.SS 4. Both options
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-t \-k alice sample.log
===== loganalyzer report: sample.log =====
Total lines      : 12
Total words      : 75
Lines with "alice" : 2
\-\-\- Log levels \-\-\-
  ERROR  : 2
  WARN   : 2
  INFO   : 5
  DEBUG  : 2
.RE
.fi
.SS 5. Help
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-h
Usage: loganalyzer [\-k keyword] [\-t] [\-h] <logfile>
  \-k keyword  count lines containing keyword
  \-t          count log levels (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG)
  \-h          show this help
.RE
.fi
.SS 6. Empty file
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer empty.log
File is empty.
.RE
.fi
.SS 7. File does not exist (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer missing.log
Error opening log file: No such file or directory
.RE
.fi
.SS 8. Bad option (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer \-z sample.log
loganalyzer: invalid option \-\- 'z'
.RE
.fi
Then prints the help text.
.SS 9. No file given (exit 1)
.nf
.RS
$ loganalyzer
Error: no log file given
.RE
.fi
Then prints the help text.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR open (2),
.BR fstat (2),
.BR getopt (3),
.BR signal (2)
